Test scenarios:
1. GET /atividades com Origin http://localhost:5500 → 200 com Access-Control-Allow-Origin igual à origem.
2. Origin não permitida (ex: http://outro.com) → sem Access-Control-Allow-Origin de valor diferente? The requirement is to allow localhost:5500. Minimal: only allow that origin. For other origins, don't echo. Actually simplest to allow only that one origin.
3. Preflight OPTIONS com Origin + Access-Control-Request-Method: GET e Access-Control-Request-Headers: x-usuario → 204 com Access-Control-Allow-Origin, Access-Control-Allow-Methods, Access-Control-Allow-Headers incluindo x-usuario.
4. Headers: Access-Control-Allow-Headers inclui X-Usuario; Access-Control-Allow-Methods inclui os métodos usados (GET, POST, PATCH). Actually methods used by project: GET, POST, PATCH, PUT (para _teste/relogio), DELETE (painel M5). Hmm. "permitir os métodos usados pelo projeto" — the API has GET, POST, PATCH; _teste uses POST, PUT, GET. M5 uses DELETE. But minimal for now: GET, POST, PATCH, PUT, DELETE? Or just the ones used currently. I'll include GET, POST, PATCH, PUT, DELETE — safe and reasonable. Actually, to be minimal and not over-invent, allow methods used in the app currently: GET, POST, PATCH, PUT. Add DELETE too since it's in contract M5... but M5 not implemented. Hmm. The requirement "permitir os métodos usados pelo projeto". The project's contract includes DELETE (painel/bloqueios). But not implemented. I'll allow "GET, POST, PATCH, PUT, DELETE". That's reasonable and minimal enough. Actually a production Express cors() uses comma-joined list. Let me just include the ones present in the contract routes: GET, POST, PATCH, PUT, DELETE. Fine.

Where to implement: in criarServidor, add middleware before routes. Handle OPTIONS: Express will match OPTIONS /route if we add app.options('*', ...) or a middleware intercepting OPTIONS and responding 204. Need to short-circuit preflight.

Implementation sketch:

```js
const ORIGEM_PERMITIDA = 'http://localhost:5500';

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  if (req.headers.origin === ORIGEM_PERMITIDA) {
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', ORIGEM_PERMITIDA);
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET, POST, PATCH, PUT, DELETE');
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Headers', 'Content-Type, X-Usuario');
  }
  if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') {
    return res.status(204).end();
  }
  next();
});
```

Wait — but careful: OPTIONS preflight from browser includes Origin header. If Origin is allowed, respond 204 with headers. If origin not allowed, still respond 204? Browsers will block. Minimal correction: only allow the exact origin. For other origins, don't set ACAO; browser blocks. Responding 204 on OPTIONS regardless is fine.

But note: should OPTIONS return 204 even without Origin? The preflight always sends Origin. Keep simple: if method OPTIONS → 204. Set CORS headers only if origin allowed.

Also the error handler for entity.parse.failed: preflight OPTIONS has no body, fine.

Now, express.json() is applied with app.use(express.json()) — OPTIONS requests have no body and no content-type, express.json will just skip. Good.

Testing with supertest: supertest OPTIONS request — app.options? Since app has route POST /atividades etc. OPTIONS /atividades would 404 if no route matches... but our middleware intercepts OPTIONS before routes and responds 204. Good, because app.use middleware runs before route handlers. Actually app.use((req,res,next)=>...) registered at top before routes — yes it runs first for all methods. Good.

But careful: the middleware order relative to express.json(). CORS middleware should be first or after? Both fine. I'll put CORS middleware early, right after `const app = express();` and before express.json? express.json parses based on content-type; OPTIONS has none. Put CORS first.

